                        "review": "As the description of the game above says, this is a spooky thrillling third-person point&click adventure.\n\nI will not reveal much of the story because the meaning of the game is discovering what’s going on around you.\n\n1)Just some tidbits:\nYou are a sort of P.I., straight coming out from the ’80/’90 with his long dark coat, sunglasses always worn even if the sun has skipped totally over the city and his tough manners and rough voice, who is trying to find out what’s happened to his girlfriend.\nRetracing some of her action, he will bump in to strange characters that sometimes will give him a hand (even secretly) sometimes will just ignore him (even if they have other plans in mind). Where is she? Is she still alive?\nPlay it and find out...\n\n2)Gameplay:\nThe red hotspot indicates the object (or character) with which you can interact. The right button of the mouse gives you some description of what you are observing (that appears at the top of the screen); some objectes require a close scrutiny when they are in your inventory (at the bottom of your screen). Some of them can also be combined.\nCollect everything, it could come in handy later (all has a purpose!)\nAt the start of the game you are given a folder and a strange coin: don’t forget to dip in to it..you never know what clues can come through!\nDuring the game you will find a map that shows the main buildings and clicking on them will move you there immediatly.\nWhen you try to use something on a red hotspot, if it’s the right thing, it will wobble..but sometimes even if it’wobbling, it will not fit..so it may be of help having an ample point of view and using your wits.\nDon’t let any “dark corner” goes unchecked because the answer.. can be in plain sight..\n\nBeing Last Half of The Darkness a saga, if you want to have the whole picture of its story, you have to start from “Shadows of the Servant” then “Beyond the Spirit’s Eye, “Tomb of Zojir” and “Society of the Serpent Moon”.\n\nI like these games because their plots are good and engaging; the puzzles and riddles are comprehensible and the gameplay is easy and neat.\n\nSo my rating for them is 8/10",
